<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vaal River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vaal River is the largest tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, flowing about 1,120 kilometres westwards from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpumalanga">Mpumalanga</a>. In the 19th century it marked the boundary between the Orange Free State to the south and the South African Republic, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, which took its name from lying beyond the Vaal. Its dams now supply water to the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vaal-river.jpg" alt="Vaal River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kierano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VaalFromN3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Vaal River rises near Breyten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpumalanga">Mpumalanga</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> and only about 240 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, and flows westwards to meet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-cape">Northern Cape</a>. It forms the border between Mpumalanga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a> and North West Province on its north bank and the Free State on its south, and is the longest river wholly within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Vaal is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> name, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>, translated from an earlier Kora Khoekhoe name. Both Vaal and the Khoekhoe ǀHai mean drab or dull, alluding to the colour of its silt-laden waters in flood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At its height in the mid-19th century the river formed the northern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a>'s Basotho kingdom, and it then became the boundary between two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics, the South African Republic and the Orange Free State. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> means beyond the Vaal, reckoned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> and Natal, the main areas of European settlement, which lay to the south.</p><p>The gold rush of the late 19th century drew people to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a>, whose growing population first used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> from the Zuurbekom Wells before the Vaal became its main source. Private schemes such as the Sivewright Concession of 1887 supplied expensive water. The Rand Water Board, founded in 1903 and fully operating from 1905, took over supply, and its works include the barrage of 1914 to 1924, the Vereeniging Pumping Station of 1924 and the Vaal Dam, completed in 1938.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Because its runoff is erratic, large dams hold its water, and transfer schemes bring more from other catchments: the Tugela-Vaal scheme was completed in 1974, and the Lesotho Highlands Water Project, under a treaty signed on 24 October 1987, was launched in 1997. The river supplies industry in the Greater Johannesburg area and part of the Free State, supports irrigation and serves about 12 million consumers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a> and nearby. About 50 kilometres are navigable, with boating and yachting around Deneysville.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vaal River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Orange Free State took shape on the high grasslands between the Orange and <b>Vaal</b> rivers, where Voortrekkers and other Dutch-speaking farmers from the Cape Colony settled from the 1830s among Griqua, Barolong, Sotho and other communities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Britain annexed Natal in 1843 and proclaimed the Orange River Sovereignty in 1848, many trekkers moved north of the <b>Vaal River</b> and settled in scattered communities around Potchefstroom, Lydenburg, Zoutpansberg and Utrecht.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sir Harry Smith proclaimed British sovereignty over the territory between the Orange and <b>Vaal</b> rivers in February 1848, hoping to control the Boers and keep peace with the Griqua and Basotho.</blockquote><a href="/state/orange-river-sovereignty">Orange River Sovereignty</a></li><li><blockquote>After driving the Ndebele north, Voortrekkers led by Andries Hendrik Potgieter founded Potchefstroom in 1838, the first European town north of the <b>Vaal River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/potchefstroom-republic">Potchefstroom Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Voortrekkers under Andries Hendrik Potgieter obtained land between the Vet and <b>Vaal</b> rivers from the Taung chief Makwana in 1836.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-winburg">Republic of Winburg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1833 the Barolong boo Seleka under Moroka II, together with Wesleyan missionaries, moved from Platberg on the <b>Vaal</b> to the mountain of Thaba Nchu, on land obtained from the Basotho.</blockquote><a href="/state/thaba-nchu">Thaba Nchu</a></li></ul></section>
